  "title": "Toronto to London fares for TripSync, live from Google Flights",
  "intro": "Every request is a live Google Flights search for the exact date asked, not a lookup in a cache, so a date nobody searched before still gets an answer. Below is one request run once: Toronto to London, one way, February 4, in Canadian dollars.",
  "resultLine": "YYZ to LHR on Feb 4, 2027: CA$481 nonstop on Air Canada. Google marks it typical for this route (usual range CA$440 to CA$520).",
  "fanout": {
    "title": "Every departure day in February",
    "note": "The REST API takes one date per request, so a month is 28 requests. PRO allows 150 a minute, so they can all run in parallel.",
    "code": "for d in $(seq -w 1 28); do\n  curl -s https://api.flightpowers.com/v1/flights/oneway \\\n    -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    -H \"x-api-key: $RAPIDAPI_KEY\" \\\n    -d \"{\\\"from_airport\\\":\\\"YYZ\\\",\\\"to_airport\\\":\\\"LHR\\\",\\\"departure_date\\\":\\\"2027-02-$d\\\",\\\"currency\\\":\\\"CAD\\\",\\\"limit\\\":5}\" &\ndone; wait"
  },
  "mcp": {
    "server": "flights",
    "prompt": "Find the cheapest one-way flight from Toronto (YYZ) to London (LHR) on any day in February 2027, in CAD, and tell me how each fare sits against Google’s usual range for the route.",
    "note": "The flights MCP server takes a date range in one call and fans it out itself, so the month above is a single prompt."
  },
  "extras": [
    "A round trip is one request to /v1/flights/roundtrip: both legs priced together, one total, one booking link.",
    "Every row carries Google’s own price range for the route and a low / typical / high verdict, as in the response above."
  ],
